# /{{VERB_INTRO}} — Meet {{PLUGIN_NAME}}

> New here? This is the one-screen orientation. Read it, then run `{{VERB_PRIME}}` to start.

## What {{PLUGIN_NAME}} is

{{PLUGIN_NAME}} is **{{ORG_NAME}}'s agent harness** — a Claude Code plugin that helps
you take work from a rough idea to a shipped, verified change, the way {{ORG_NAME}}
actually works.

It is **neutral by design**: it does not hardcode {{ORG_NAME}}'s process. It reads the
operating model from the org brain (`{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`) at the start of each session,
so the process can evolve in the wiki without re-releasing this plugin.

## How to think about it

- **The wiki is the brain.** `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}` holds the operating model, the rules,
  the decisions, and the hard-won learnings. That's where the process lives.
- **{{PLUGIN_NAME}} is the hands.** It reads the brain and drives the flow.
- **You are the top of the loop.** It's dumb pipes + smart endpoints: nothing runs
  fire-and-forget. Scope changes, design decisions, and anything ambiguous come back
  to you.

## What you can do

Invoke these as `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:<verb>`:

| Verb | Use it to… | Set effort first |
|---|---|---|
| `{{VERB_SETUP}}` | One-time bootstrap (clone the brain, point the harness at it). | low |
| `{{VERB_PRIME}}` | Calibrate the session — load the current process + rules from the wiki. **Run first.** | low–med |
| `{{VERB_INCEPTION}}` | Kick off a brand-new project — map repos + architecture, originate the epic/story backlog. | **high** (xhigh for a big greenfield) |
| `{{VERB_REFINE}}` | Turn a rough ticket into an agent-ready spec (constraints, SLOs, acceptance test). | **high** |
| `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` | Break an agent-ready story into scoped tasks and run them through agents. | medium |
| `{{VERB_WIKI}}` | Read the org brain (`query`) or contribute a decision/learning (`contribute`). | medium |
| `{{VERB_HANDOFF}}` | Cycle a long session cleanly without losing your place. | medium |

### A note on effort

The harness can't set your session effort for you — **you** set it (`/effort <level>`)
before invoking a skill, which is why the recommended level is in the table above. The
rule of thumb: **effort tracks judgment, not length.** `{{VERB_INCEPTION}}` and `{{VERB_REFINE}}` are the
deep-thinking steps (structure a fuzzy problem, design the acceptance test) — run them
**high**. `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` is mostly *dispatch + monitor* — the heavy reasoning happens inside
the agents it spawns (which run at their own configured depth, e.g. opus/high), so the
orchestrating session itself can sit at **medium**. `{{VERB_SETUP}}`/`{{VERB_PRIME}}` are
light. *(the `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}` level is a starting point — bump it if decomposition feels
under-thought.)*

> **Why the agents don't appear here:** `implementer` / `reviewer` / `{{VERB_GATE}}` are **roles
> instantiated by Workflow stages**, not skills you invoke. Their model + effort are
> fixed in their definitions — you never set effort for them by hand.

## The shape of the work

```
  {{VERB_INCEPTION}}  ──►  {{VERB_REFINE}}  ──►  {{VERB_EXECUTE}}  ──►  {{VERB_GATE}}
  (new project)   (idea → agent-ready)   (agents do the work)   (validated pre-merge)
```

`{{VERB_INCEPTION}}` is once per project (map repos + originate the backlog); `{{VERB_REFINE}}` and `{{VERB_EXECUTE}}`
are per story, yours to drive. The gates and agent isolation keep the work honest along
the way.

## Start here

**First time on this machine?** Make a fresh empty directory, start Claude in it, and
run setup once — it clones the org brain (`{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}`) right there and points the
harness at it:

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_SETUP}}
```

**Every session after that:**

```
/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_PRIME}}
```

`{{VERB_PRIME}}` reads the operating model + rules + compliance from `{{ORG_WIKI_NAME}}` and tells
you, in a few lines, what process is in force and what to do next. Everything else
builds on it. (If you run `{{VERB_PRIME}}` before `{{VERB_SETUP}}`, it'll tell you to set up first.)

Want the real rules, not just this summary? They live in the wiki — `{{VERB_PRIME}}` loads
them, and `/{{PLUGIN_NAME}}:{{VERB_WIKI}} query <question>` answers from them on demand.

